 is all of the documentation is  there  if you'd like to take a look  a second major uh  lesson was  in the next slide was to widen the  participant base  in the case of  delaware we dealt with  mainly state agencies  it's become quite clear  working in other jurisdictions that  we need to widen who can participate in  this and that also means that we have to  have the kind of expertise needed for  uh the um  different kinds of uh entities to  participate so  this is the uh the range of participants  that we are now marketing to in  pennsylvania  next slide please  we also  make sure that we can customize the  measures for the individual participant  that is a really key  factor there is no one-size-fits-all  i think many of you know that  a couple of measures such as lighting  have often been  used as the basis for these kinds of  financings but it typically is the case  that  uh these public and non-profit  participants are really looking for a  series of measures that can be  implemented  um  and uh through that implementation  process that a more comprehensive  approach to their energy uh requirements  can be had so  we basically include all of the standard  conservation measures but we also  include distributed generation on site  uh we have worked in a variety of  different renewable energy sources  including  solar we  have also worked on a variety of options  we're currently looking at a microgrid  in one of our  project opportunities and  all of this with the key requirement  that  whatever the investment is that it can  create a stream of energy and water bill  savings sufficient to fully  finance the investment  finally a major thing that we learned  out of all of this next slide please  monitoring your verification a lot of  times unless you are talking about a  large  entity that has staff on hand to be able  to handle this  m v can kind of be part of that  participant in headlight syndrome  folks don't find it easy to to  understand or  to utilize to their own benefits so we  have done two things here  first of all the monitoring and  verification process that's in our  documentation  uh is diagnostic  it provides advance indication to the  both the participant and the company  that has signed the guaranteed savings  agreement whether the guarantee is going  to be met and it provides the basis for  cures  to then be developed over the course of  the year in order to assure  uh that the  that the guarantee is met  rather than arguing over fine details of  uh  how to measure building performance and  so on we have removed that  uh particular problem in the past with  mmv instead the program itself sets the  uh  a schedule of energy and water prices  over the 20-year period  the program  through the advice of the free team sets  it conservatively  and that price uh vector is then  multiplied by the  physical unit  uh savings that  are provided by the  uh the participating uh contractors and  uh that then defines for each and every  year what the dollar savings must be  and  the question now is only how can we make  sure that we meet that most cost  effectively we use all the standard  m v protocol but i want to emphasize  that  the approach that we take here  essentially removes  what has been passed practice of arguing  over  finer details on methodology and instead  simply diagnoses what needs to be done  because the free team is there as part  of the both the negotiation process and  the post-construction process  we are able to turn this particular item  away from what has been  not necessarily positive experience for  some uh we've now been able to turn this  into a step that actually  is a positive part of a transaction of  this kind  all right the next two slides my final  two slides just briefly go through the  process by which  one  participates in a program like this this  is the  the  pensef particular eight-step process we  we obtain a letter of interest from  participants and we gather a sufficient  number of letters to  then produce education days because  pennsylvania is a large state we  do these educational efforts in  multiple locations  where we go through the financing in  detail and  the  participants can ask  questions and then we break down and  work with them afterwards  on how to get  a project development process started  for them  we assist them throughout all of the  steps we  help them to get the kind of data that  will be needed for  a pre-contract off audit that we offer  in the process this is no cost to the  to the participant  and  we have a fairly seamless way in which  billing and  other kinds of data that's needed uh  come to the  the escos that are pre-qualified  and we then  use that pre-contract audit to give  participants an idea of what kind of  project or project they might want to  think about  if they next slide please if they like  the  what they see  we move to a next step which is to then  have an investment grade audit done the  investment grade audit must deliver at  least 90 percent  of the  savings identified in the pre-contract  audit uh this is to prevent gaming  of  participants in this particular process  we then move uh with the participants  through that iga the investment grade  audit if they  uh find enough in there that they want  to  develop into what we call a project we  then  begin to package that project into a  guaranteed savings agreement again the  documentation was all standardized all  participants  all participating companies  use the same documents for this purpose  and we back in that guaranteed savings  agreement with a detailed  um  monitoring and verification  plan  next step is then for the  the  participant to accept the proposal  spells out the guarantee they get to  indicate they get to choose  and we customize we give them different  analytics on  options they might have so that they can  look at what their uh debt payments  against uh savings would look like and  if they want to front end stuff they can  do that if they want to back in we can  do that if they want levelize that  service  we can do that um  at that point we then move to the  performance of the retro fit work  this does include on-site generation we  have done renewable energy projects as  part of this as well  and then  as i mentioned we then stay with the  process after construction to make sure  that  the monitoring and verification  plans actually show to the satisfaction  of the participants that they are  realizing the dollar savings not simply  physical unit savings uh that they uh  had anticipated  um and that's really the basics of uh of  the  program  uh i've included i just mentioned i've  included  i won't go into them but some  additional information on the new effort  that we're working on which is to now  create what we  regard as solar city platforms and which  investment for example in  solar technology can take place on a  citywide basis and we've just completed  an analysis for  six cities  uh new york london  amsterdam munich tokyo and seoul to show  how  you can combine policy technology  financing we did all of this as bond  financing  and so i think there's a lot of exciting  opportunities coming up on this kind of  scale and i just wanted to  let you know about that particular item  and i think that's all that i have i'm  trying to go quickly so that we don't  chew up the entire time of the  webinar for presentations thank you very  much  hi this is emily um and i'm hoping that  you can hear me i know that the yale  team who's going to be moderating our q  a has been having some technical  difficulties so yale if you you can hear  me  we actually can't hear you hi hi emily  can you hear me now emily i can this is  birch hunter i can hear you  emily can you hear me now yes there you  are michael hi  myself uh okay so we had we've had some  good questions from the uh the audience  um i'll start off with adam and alison  uh we've had a few questions from  patrick and carol  uh and a couple of others uh really  about what's in it for the utilities to  participate in on bill financing  are they incentivized are they  charged a fee  and if not how would um you know being  charged  or  charging a fee for that process affect  the economics of on bill financing  uh  so i'll take that quickly this is adam  the um we we do pay the utilities uh  monthly fee craft three pays the  utilities a monthly fee for the cost of  providing on bill servicing and it's  it's it's quite low  um  uh we've been recently talking with them  about  um what they should be charging and and  it's it's a good healthy negotiation and  i think they're they believe that  uh this has been a benefit to their  customers they see happy customers  um  and and so they um they're working with  us on the fee  uh interestingly in oregon on bill  repayment  is uh by the ious is more or less  required by law by state law  so when we started this the utilities  were  compelled to have a conversation with  ourselves uh the department of oregon  department of energy and the energy  trust of oregon which is a strong  partner in this and has been  um  helping uh the conversations with the  utilities  from the beginning and now plays a more  integral role in terms of contracting  um  i would say though that in the last six  months  uh conversations between craft three  energy trust and the participating  utilities have turned more to a  voluntary  uh um  uh  perspective so we are now talking with  them about  uh expanding on bill lending programs uh  to their customers and they they are  coming to us and asking us to help them  develop that feature um both us and at  the energy trust of oregon so that has  been very interesting i think that is  proof  uh that  they  um  view this as a positive feature  great that that makes a lot of sense um  okay so the next question is from roger  and it's for birth  and the question is really around uh the  role that sea pace um specifically for  