ot uh another couple of  questions for adam and allison these  come from steve and carol  really around how you would go about  setting up a non-bill financing program  in a new jurisdiction or with a new  utility  what approach would you take what sort  of conditions need to be in place for  that to be successful  um  well  i i would  i would first of all say that our  organization craft three we're a finance  institution and we um we speak uh sort  of a novice utility speak  we have been very lucky  in oregon especially to have  the energy trust of oregon which is a  an organization that was created  by state law to work with utilities on  conservation and renewable energy to  work with the utilities and us to  set up a number of these programs so um  from our perspective as a as a loan  originator i think it's really important  to  uh  um  excuse me i'm sorry  i it's very important as a loan  originator to have a partner that works  uh in in utility land and can help  translate some of the more complex  questions about regulations so i i would  i would look for someone at the state  department of energy in the states that  you work or if you're lucky enough to  have um  one of the third party organizations  in in states like vermont and in  wisconsin that would be very helpful  other than that i think it's  about crafting agreements that are very  clear  in terms of  risk mitigation and protection for the  utilities so our agreements  with utilities and energy trust  spell out very carefully what we  um what we do with their customers and  what they are not obligated to worry  about when in terms of loan losses and i  think that's a huge piece  or defaults  or delinquencies that's a huge piece of  what we do because ultimately they just  want to process the payments and deliver  their  customers a service  okay thanks for that uh okay so the next  question is from steve for john  and he asks how delaware's seu projects  have actually performed so far  and he was 